4. Emperor Debate

Victor Tsai, ‘30, Leo Wang, ‘30
Who’s the best Roman emperor? If you’re not in 8th grade, you probably have no clue or you don’t really care. But for the Class of 2030, this question has been the backbone of Latin class the entire year. The 8th grade Latin X contest is a (not-so) friendly year-long competition in which multiple teams from each Latin class are each assigned a different Roman emperor. It’s all luck-based too, since emperors are chosen through a raffle, meaning you could have first-pick, or be left with someone like Commodus. Then, throughout the year, teams research, defend, and absolutely glaze the living daylights out of their emperor—whether or not they think they’re even good—in order to earn some points for their power rankings. 
There’s multiple major events to earn MASSIVE points and climb the ladder to hopefully have the most points at the end of the year, like the Argumentum Videos, Evening with the Emperors, or the grand finale: The Emperor Debates. Starting with the class rounds, each class holds debates between the emperors within the class. Each team attempts to answer questions regarding their emperor’s achievements, and why they should be brought back from the Underworld. Then, when both sides have answered their respective questions, they are let off the leash and finally have the ability to criticize the opposing emperor and formulate rebuttals. The winner of the class debate is nominated to then head to the big stage, where the OFFICIAL EMPEROR DEBATE will occur.
Then, the top performers from each class will go head to head, battling it out to prove once and for all which Roman emperor is the best of them all. The winner of the debate has the potential to climb quite a lot of the ladder, meaning those in first might not stay in first for long, which makes the debate super high stakes.
At the end of the day though, the entire goal of this year-long competition is not to fester hatred (although it definitely did), but rather to allow students to study both sides of ancient Rome’s culture; the good, the bad, and the ugly. Good luck to all of the teams competing in this final debate!
Ad Astra Per Aspera!

5. Musc in the Parks

By Leo Wang (‘30) and Victor Tsai (‘30) The spring concerts are over, and performers can finally breathe a sigh of relief. Or can they? Most people outside the music world might assume music classes relax during the final weeks of school. Think again! Cue Music in the Parks, an annual mid-May event where different schools from across the region compete through musical talent. Performers are judged on everything from crisp rhythms to musicality, including tone quality, intonation, technique, and that magical “wow” factor. EA sends a quite impressive lineup to the event: the Symphonic Band, Jazz Band, Middle School Orchestra, Chamber Ensemble, Cantores, and MS Singers; most of the musical groups are participating. Students perform for two judges each, who score them using a set rubric. Afterwards, students head off to enjoy a few hours of fun at Hershey Park and unwind from the nerves of the performance. But the real event comes next: everyone gathers at Hershey Stadium for awards, where 1st, 2nd, and 3rd place winners, along with everyone else, receive trophies and feedback. Once the excitement is over, students are free to ride roller coasters and other adrenaline-pumping rides, enjoy sweet treats and snacks, and compete in the arcades for four glorious hours. As the day winds down, everyone meets at the park entrance. Another year of Music in the Parks is in the books, and everyone gets to take a nice, relaxing trip back on the buses.

