7. PJAS States

PJAS States
Ellen Le, ‘30, Leo Wang, ‘30
	There was an article in the last edition that detailed everything about the Pennsylvania Junior Academy of Science (PJAS) Regionals and the Middle School STEM Discovery elective. Now, we’re here for a follow-up into PJAS States! 
Every year, the PJAS State Competition takes place at the main campus of Penn State University in University Park, which is about a 3 hour drive from EA. With the arrival of over 3,000+ competitors, teachers, and judges, PJAS States is one of the largest scientific gatherings in the Pennsylvania area, spanning from Middle School all the way through Upper School. There, students can enjoy ice cream at the famously delicious creamery, visit Penn State campus landmarks, converse with fellow students, and even have the option to stay overnight on Sunday night to really soak up the “vibe.” 
	Following the exact same format as the Regional competition, you are competing against a rubric instead of other participants—so multiple people can get first-place awards if they do well enough. Although the rubric is the same, the people doing the judging will often have higher expectations, so all of the hard work put into Regionals should be continued going into States.
This year at Regionals, Ellen Le, Elise Markle, Robbie Deirmegian, Jackson Rogers, and Leo Wang all earned a first-place award and are eligible to compete at States on May 18. While their peers attend school that Monday, these students will be presenting their best work at PJAS States.
Once again, no matter the results, each participant has put an exceptional amount of effort into perfecting both their experiment and their presentation, and the entire EA community is excited to see what they’ll accomplish. Best of luck to those competing this year!

8. Student Council Events

StuCo Events:
By: Sara Adibi

Easter Egg Hunt:

Every spring, Student Council members help set up and run the annual easter egg hunt. 6th, 7th, and 8th grade students all participate in the easter egg hunt, trading in eggs they collect for candy. Each grade also has the chance to find the golden egg, carefully hidden by a student council representative. If a student finds a golden egg, they get a chocolate bunny in return. Student Council works hard to get the school ready for the easter egg hunt, scattering eggs around the school and finding places to hide the golden egg. They also regulate the event and clean up the broken eggs afterwards.

Kickball tournament:

Student Council also hosted a kickball tournament for all students joining EA middle school next year, whether it was rising sixth graders or new 7th or 8th grade students. This event was supposed to help new students build connections with student council representatives and other incoming sutdents. Tess McNeely ‘31, a seventh grade student council member, states, “It was helpful in welcoming students because it helped introduce them to other incoming students. It also gave them the chance to meet other new students going into new grades. Hopefully, they all got a chance to talk to other people which will make their transition into EA easier with some new friends already there.” Jameson Graneri ‘31, another 7th grade Student Council representative, adds on, “The student kickball tournament heped welcome new students to EA Middle School by giving everyone a fn and relaxed way to meet people outside of class. Since students were working together on teams and cheering each other on, it made it easier for new students to start conversations and feel included.”

Teacher Appreciation:

Lastly, Student Council helped set up two events to demonstrate teacher appreciation. The first of these events was Dress Up Like A Teacher day. During this day, students have the opportunity to dress up as any teacher they’d like, coining their signature styles. This helps teachers feel acknowledged by their students and always allows them to laugh to see how students dress like them. Finally, Student Council ran an event in which they gave students prompts to give to teachers. These included prompts like ‘give this to the teacher who works hard each day’, ‘give this to the teacher who makes every class fun’, or ‘give this to the teacher who makes you smile’. These types of prompts help teachers feel acknowledged for the hard work they put into teaching. There were also sillier prompts to make teachers smile such as ‘give this to the teacher most likely to take over the world’, ‘give this to the teacher most likely to survive the zombie apocalypse’, ‘give this to the teacher with the best phrases’, etc. Jameson Graneri states, “The event where we recorded students giving teachers the prompts helped to show the teachers that they were valued and recognized. It showed that students took time to celebrate them in a personal way.”


Student Council runs many events throughout the year, and this spring we helped make new students and teachers feel appreciated.

